  "worked_solution": "First, we compute ${C}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${C}^{2}=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${C}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{3} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{C}^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
